  - [38;5;153msrc/index.js[39m + [38;5;153msrc/utils.js[39m — a separate Node entry point ([38;5;153mmain[39m in package.json) with one [38;5;153mconsole.log[39m
  - No dependencies, no test setup, no build step, no linter

  One flag before questions: [38;5;153mapp.js:5[39m already logs the username on every login attempt, and the submit handler reads a
  password into scope. "Debug production issues" usually means shipping logs somewhere. The moment logs leave the
  browser, that line becomes credential-adjacent PII in a remote store. Redaction needs to be a designed-in part of
  this, not an afterthought — I'll carry it through the design.

  [1mThe fork, first question:[0m "the app" could be either of two surfaces, and they need genuinely different machinery.
  Browser logs are useless for production debugging unless they're transmitted somewhere (you can't read a user's
  devtools), so that path implies a transport, a batching/failure policy, and a privacy posture. Node logs go to stdout
  and are collected by whatever runs the process — no transport, no privacy surface, far cheaper. Picking both means
  building the shared core so it can serve two sinks.

  - [1mBrowser only[0m — buys real production visibility into the flow that actually has users; costs a transport + redaction
    design; rules out "just print it" simplicity.
  - [1mNode only[0m — cheapest by a wide margin, structured JSON to stdout, done; rules out seeing anything that happens in
    the user's browser, which is where your login bug would live.
  - [1mBoth, shared core[0m — one logger API, two sinks; costs an abstraction boundary up front, but avoids rewriting the
    browser side later if you start with Node.

  My recommendation is [1mbrowser only[0m. [38;5;153msrc/index.js[39m is a three-line greeter with nothing to debug; the login flow is the
  only thing here that could plausibly have a production issue. Starting browser-first also forces the redaction
  question immediately, which is the part that's expensive to retrofit.
